From 203f939aeb1cd66711289bf00e0d4e7f05e52028 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Haofeng Li <lihaofeng@kylinos.cn>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 10:07:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] ksmbd: initialize parsed stream types

An SMB named stream without an explicit type, such as "file:stream",
implicitly has type $DATA.  The parser does not update s_type for this
form.  It sets it only if a second colon introduces an explicit type.

The create caller happens to initialize its variable, but the rename
caller passes an uninitialized stack value on to the xattr stream-name
builder.  Its result can therefore depend on stale stack contents.

Initialize the output to DATA_STREAM when parsing begins.  Explicit
$DATA and $INDEX_ALLOCATION suffixes are still parsed normally.

Reachable over the network from an authenticated client:

  SMB2 SET_INFO
    -> FILE_RENAME_INFORMATION -> set_rename_info() -> smb2_rename()
    -> parse_stream_name()                       (s_type left unwritten)
    -> ksmbd_vfs_xattr_stream_name()             (consumes s_type)

The implicit-type rename form (":stream" or "dir/:stream") is what
reaches the builder; "file:stream" above illustrates the parser
semantics rather than the exact request name.

Signed-off-by: Haofeng Li <lihaofeng@kylinos.cn>
Reviewed-by: ChenXiaoSong <chenxiaosong@kylinos.cn>
---
 fs/smb/server/misc.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/fs/smb/server/misc.c b/fs/smb/server/misc.c
index 2dd91c9e956a..dcecb6631fe9 100644
--- a/fs/smb/server/misc.c
+++ b/fs/smb/server/misc.c
@@ -124,6 +124,7 @@ int parse_stream_name(char *filename, char **stream_name, int *s_type)
 	bool has_stream_type = false;
 
 	*stream_name = NULL;
+	*s_type = DATA_STREAM;
 	s_name = filename;
 	filename = strsep(&s_name, ":");
 	ksmbd_debug(SMB, "filename : %s, streams : %s\n", filename, s_name);
